CIDER_TRACE_DICTKEY, which says whether a key was never produced or produced empty.

A keyed attribute store is how several applications model their objects, and the two failures look
identical from the caller: the accessor answers nil either way. This watches named keys, comma
separated, on BOTH sides. On a read miss it lists what the dictionary does hold, which is the line
that answers the question.

It found the answer for iA Writer in one run. The library rows say Updating... because the row
subtitle is [[self excerptProvider] value], -[IALibraryItem excerptProvider] is
[store objectForKeyedSubscript:@"excerptProvider"], and the store is an eleven key dictionary
holding treeIndex, indexed, annotated, dateCreated, locationIdentifier, identifier, treeLevel,
kind, indexRow, treeExpanded and dateModified. excerptProvider is NEVER ONE OF THEM. The excerpt is
a separate property, selected as content_excerpt(Content.body) AS excerpt, which is a custom SQLite
function in a property set the item fetch does not ask for.

TWO THINGS THIS ENCODES:

respondsToSelector rather than isKindOfClass. CoreFoundation is BELOW Foundation and cannot
reference _OBJC_CLASS_$_NSString; the first version linked against it and the build failed at the
link step rather than the compile.

description then UTF8String for the value, because a class name does not distinguish 0 from 1 and
the NSNumber class symbol is as unreachable from here as NSString's.

THE WRITE SIDE HAS A BLIND SPOT and it must be stated with any result: an immutable dictionary
built through initWithObjects:forKeys:count: never passes setObject:forKey:, so no SET line is
printed for it. Prove the write side can speak before reading its silence; watching dateFormat and
dateStyle is a control that fires on any application that formats a date.
